    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Can you go into a bit more detail on the chemical mechanism? Specifically, how the oxidation states of iron and manganese change in relation to the pH shift and how that affects the ions' ability to move through the soil solution to the roots?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In this pH range, a shift from more acidic conditions to alkalinity (toward 7.0) forces iron and manganese into stable trivalent (Fe³⁺/Mn³⁺) oxidation states, making them chemically less soluble. At lower pH levels near 5.5, these metals exist as soluble divalent ions (Fe²⁺/Mn²⁺) or simple cations that can easily diffuse through the soil solution to reach root halotychia. When the oxidation state changes to the less soluble forms near neutral pH, they precipitate as insoluble compounds like oxides or hydroxides, physically blocking their movement through the soil pore spaces. This precipitation creates a diffusion barrier where the ions cannot migrate to the root zone, preventing uptake even in soils with high nutrient reserves.",
      "source": "target",
